## Thought
The task is fairly explicit, but a few terms could be read more than one way.

- "U.S. Census Bureau survey estimates for income" does not name a survey. The ACS 1-year, the CPS ASEC and SIPP are all plausible. The Census Bureau's "real median household income" headline figures come from the CPS ASEC. State-level yearly comparisons are often taken from the ACS 1-year. The task asks the answerer to choose and justify, so the survey name is ambiguous.
- "a stated official price index from BLS" could be CPI-U, CPI-U-RS (the Census Bureau's own research series, which is published by BLS), or C-CPI-U. The PCE index is not a BLS series, so it is unlikely. "Annual average" versus a December-to-December value is a further ambiguity.
- "Indiana" is clearly the state. The only other reading is the Indiana Business Research Center or a metro area, which is very unlikely, so I leave it out.
- "2015 and 2023": calendar year versus survey year is a minor point, so I leave it out. The 2020 ACS 1-year was not released in standard form, but that does not affect these endpoints.
- "2023 dollars" is clear.
- "nominal values" is clear.

